ore than that but that would  have been a lot of a lot of tweets to  pull but basically I had to first sort  through this is referring back to  another function that I had before I  could go back to it real quick the sort  emojis function right here all this is  really doing it it's just sorting the  emojis based on the ordering of that  list that I had from before based on  their net sentiment scores so first it's  taking those that list of just numerical  values for each emoji it's sorting those  and then it's flipping it's also sorting  that list of emojis on based on the same  ordering of the other lists that I had  that way you can get a you you can sort  both both the emojis list and the list  of actual scores so that they are  aligned with each other so basically by  running this I mean it's gonna give me  the same output but that just then  allows me to go and create a bar chart  that isn't just a bunch it's not just  going up and down all over the place you  get kind of a progress a continual  progression from the most negative to  most positive and like I said these are  very hard and small small and hard to  see but the most positive one is  actually it's an emoji with stars in its  eyes and actually not not really ironic  but that's actually the one that was  right here which is right next to the  most negative one but that one was I  guess in that night by no surprise  positive but the second most positive  one is actually in angry emoji which is  this guy right here so again looking at  potentially how these are used in a  sarcastic sense or sense that I guess  doesn't make do most people who don't  use these forms of social media now if  you want to look at kind of more of a  cellular or a table I also just set up a  function for creating a data set because  if you want to take a look at well you  know a pie chart is great for  visualization and a bar charts great for  like looking at all the data in a very  you know coherent way or a way that just  r